Back in March, henley (henley@tonys.ecology.umn.edu) posted
numbers for a portable benchmark he had developed for single, SMP and
MPP systems. The following are the top-end results he came up with.
If you wanted to contact him, he likely could provide more details:

> Cray T3E (16x375 21164, 8K L1, 96K L2)    :1338 (84*16)
> Cray T3E (4x375MHz 21164, 8K L1, 96K L2)  : 317 (79*4)
> HP K460 1-way (180Mhz PA-8000)            : 260
> tweaked code on 500MHz Alpha              : 209
> quad ppro 200Mhz/512KB                    : 205 (52*4)
> 500MHz Alpha (21164)                      : 177
> 400Mhz Alpha(feedback-driven-optimization): 173
> SGI Origin2000 (single CPU)               : 148
> dual ppro 233MHz/512K                     : 125 (63*2)
> SGI Origin 200, 180 MHz, 1 MB L2          : 123 
> Alpha 433 Mhz (21164)(Linux)              : 113
> 333MHz Alpha (21164)                      : 113
> Dell 200Mhz dual ppro (256k cache)        : 109 (55*2)

As for the hardware compatibility questions, it is system dependent.

Standard multisync monitors should work fine on PC164-based
Alphas (like those sold by Enorex and Microway); and on the
the Digital Personal Workstation II's -- the 600a/500a/433a
and 600au/500au/433au.

The PWII's use SDRAM, (3.3V SDRAM parts on 168-pin DIMM modules);
the PC164 boxes use DRAM. Don't know whether you could mix and match
with a PPro, but it's commodity memory widely available.

For Alpha NT supported hardware, check out

   http://www.microsoft.com/isapi/hwtest/hsearchn4.idc


  -doug



