Steve Beresh (beresh@stokes.ae.utexas.edu) wrote:
> We're looking at purchasing an alpha machine to run linux, but the only
> serious hangup is the lack of a fortran compiler in the standard linux
> distributions (Red Hat, for example).  I understand that g77 is
> reasonably reliable, despite still being in the beta stage, but I also
> see that it has not been ported to linux/alpha.

> f2c has its own problems, particularly for code development as opposed
> to just trying to get a working executable.

> Can anyone recommend an f77 compiler that would work in linux/alpha?

I know this is surely heresy in a group like this, but you may, at
least temporarily, want to consider a dual boot system running NT
also.  Digital's Alpha-NT compiler is quite good.  Their web page 
now shows plots claiming that fortran benchmarks on Alpha-NT run
at ~92% of Alpha-Unix (Digital Unix).  I personally use it on an
ev5 Alpha NT and am quite pleased with the performance running plasma 
simulations - it blows our UltraSparc running Solaris away for a fraction
of the cost.  

You can get the Hamilton csh program for Alpha NT and pretend it's not 
NT like I do.  (It sure would be great if DEC would just make their compiler 
run on Linux, though.)  

