Claus Denk <denk@cica.es> writes:

> If I go and compile my fortran source with the DU f77 compiler
> in order to create a .s (assembly) file, change the .s file
> a little bit so that the gnu assembler will compile it on
> a linux system, is it legal to distribute the resulting object
> code (or the resulting .s files) (no DU libraries are linked) ? 

If you're planning anything other than a trivial example of this,
say, if you're thinking of making wide distributions to others
of this, it's best to hire a lawyer.

But, I'd guess that if the Digital UNIX f77 license does not
contain any language prohibiting such use of the assembly output,
you'd be fine.

Essentially, the .s file is *typically* a transformation of the
input which, in this case, you say belongs to you, so the transformation
also belongs to you, even though it is performed by someone else's
transformer (DU's f77).

However, there can be exceptions to this.  You could have signed
(or be considered to have signed) an agreement saying you wouldn't
do this sort of thing; DU's f77 could be inserting into the .s file
a substantial work that is copyrighted by Digital (or someone other
than you); and perhaps others that I can't think of right now.

(Also, this view of source->transformer->output can break down when
it comes to software.  E.g. if you wrote Fortran code to do something
the compiler decided it could replace wholesale, or substantially,
with copyrighted code *directly* inserted into your .s file from
a library -- "library" in the more generic sense of the term than
a UNIX libfoo.a file here -- then the picture isn't so clear, so
it's best to examine the .s file and be sure it has no copyright
notices in it, or other such disclaimers.)

Basically, I'd say that if you looked for explicit claims that any
of the above exceptions were the case -- e.g. your DU f77 license
agreement saying you can't just distribute a .s without taking
other steps, or the .s file containing a copyright notice -- you'd
be fine.  If it turns out Digital, or someone else, later claims
you're distributing something of theirs, you could probably just
stop doing it without having much in the way of liability, since
you looked for prohibitions in output that was reasonably expected
to belong to you (the copyright holder of the Fortran code) anyway,
and found none.  (Berne convention, etc.)

But, again, if this is more than just a trivial example Digital is
highly unlikely to care about, hire a lawyer.
-- 

