Hi there.

First of all I'd like to thank Jay at DEC and W. Dreyer at Quant-X,
they really tried hard to get this sorted out. All you others too, of
course, but nobody really had a chance.

I found the solution to the problem I've been having ever since
I upgraded my "cheap VOBIS machine" with an IBM DCAS 34330
SCSI disk on an Adaptec 2940UW controller (PC164 w/64meg RAM,
21164@500MHz, 3.8GB WD Caviar disk, IDE CD-ROM,Elsa Winner
2000avi/3d graphics).

Probably this is an issue for VOBIS customers only because they don't
really know ANYTHING about computers (which is why others couldn't
really help - hard to pretend to know nothing if you DO).

So. There's a jumper on the mainboard to set the system clock divisor,
in the users manual it's labeled J30. On a 500MHz machine, IRQ[0,1,2,3]
should NOT be jumpered. Mine was set to IRQ2, which is correct for a
366MHz CPU. For 500MHz, this leads to a frequency >45MHz on the
board, which the Adaptec couldn't cope with.

Actually I don't understand how my machine could have worked at
all (PCI specs are 33MHz, or did I miss something here?).

If you got your Alpha at VOBIS in Germany, check the board. I guess
this might solve some other seemingly unrelated probs, too.

cu, 

Jo

