In article <w53zprispqn.fsf@mars.i-have-a-misconfigured-system-so-shoot-me>,
Jo Hausmann  <joe@mars.neuro.uni-oldenburg.de> wrote:
>366MHz CPU. For 500MHz, this leads to a frequency >45MHz on the
>board, which the Adaptec couldn't cope with.
>
>Actually I don't understand how my machine could have worked at
>all (PCI specs are 33MHz, or did I miss something here?).

The components in a computer are often capable of running at a higher rate
than the specs; only this behaviour is not guaranteed by the constructor (so
you cannot blame him if your PCI card does not run above 33 MHz).

45 MHz may be too off-specs, but PCI cards almost always work at 37.5 MHz,
as this speed is what you find in a PC with a Cyrix p200+ processor (internal
speed 150 MHz, memory bus at 75 MHz, and the PCI bus speed is typically at
half the speed of the memory bus in PC motherboards). Adaptec scsi cards
have once been rumored to be a bit less tolerant to overclocking than other
PCI cards.

	--Thomas Pornin



