Theo Van Dinter (felicity@eclectic.kluge.net) wrote:
: 
: the question I posed was actually more about the motherboards used, rather
: than the chip (since they're standard) ...

Boards are standard as well.  It is actually possible to make a machine
which will run Digital Unix, NT and Linux although this implies
some ingenuity (DU boots from SRM console, NT requires ARC, Linux will
boot from both but disk partitioning type in these two schemes is different).

: As for the problem ...
: basically I have a bunch of processes which are long run/high computation
: (int. based, not fp.) for mathematical modeling of systems.

Then you should fly with Linux. Good floating-point libraries for Linux/Alpha
are a weaker point; such that could compete with DU are still not there
but many floating point code runs succesfuly with Linux (for a price
of a multiple user DU licence you can put up the second machine :-).

If you are interested in optimization issues and not afraid to get your
hands a bit dirty with a code you should look at this paper:

http://www.linuxexpo.org/1997/after/papers/davidm/paper-html/

by David Mosberger-Tang.  It has some interesting numbers and comparisons.

: I was thinking perhaps the ISA/PCI motherboards which seem to be prevelant
: are what makes the "low-end" designation ...

ISA ports or Alpha boards normally exist, but they are a "side-show".
Designs used by Digital are PCI as well.  Bottlenecks are normally
memory access path and cache sizes - they do not depend on which OS
you just happen to use but on a board type.

There are some Alpha machines which are not supported by Linux
(different bus architecture; but you may apparently run FreeBSD on these :-).

  Michal




