On 23 Jul 1997, Christopher L. Mikkelson wrote:
> Yeah, DEC is pulling some strange marketing with their Alphas.  Apparently,
> they make two varieties of Alpha, which differ in PAL code.  There are NT
> models and Unix models.  Unix models run DEC Unix, and you can probably guess
> what NT models are meant to run.

  I think that is referring to the two types of boot console firmware: SRM and
ARC.  ARC (Advanced RISC Console or some such thing) is a child of Microsoft's
attempt to be non-Intel dependent.  It is designed to boot NT, can be made to
boot Linux, and has a menu-driven interface and a few of leftovers from the
way DOS and NT do things.  SRM (System Reference Manual, IIRC) is a strictly
DEC thing.  It gives you a boot prompt, where you can type debugging commands
and boot an OS.  Digital UNIX and Linux can both be booted this way. 

  You have either one console or the other, but you can switch between the two
on some models.  As I understand it, some Alpha motherboards can only support
one type, and others can do both with firmware upgrades.

> <soapbox>
> Why on earth is DEC doing this?  Their Unix-only Alphas cost significantly
> more than NT alphas, to the point where vendors (Microway anyway) will list
> prices only for the NT models.

  The reason for ARC vs SRM is NT will only work with ARC, but ARC is designed
around MS OSes and doesn't like others.  As for why one costs more, I believe
DEC's policy is not to sell an Alpha machine without a license for either NT
or DU.  Since the DU license carries a four-figure price tag, it would raise
the price of said machine quite a bit over the NT one.  But don't quote me,
this is just a guess.  :)

> As I see it, DEC has been, for lack of a better term, "assimilated."  They
> have turned into JANTR (Just Another NT Reseller).

  I've never been very impressed by DEC's marketing practices.  With the
performance of the Alpha architecture, coupled with some of the first
reasonable prices seen in the professional workstation market, not to mention
support for NT and the FX!32 Intel-emulator, DEC should have been able to make
a real killing.  Instead, most people say, "Alpha?  What's that?" 

