g77-0.5.20 is the first public version of g77 that has Linux/Alpha
support, but it builds more easily for older (and, I gather, buggier)
ECOFF systems than the newer ELF systems.  Plus, 0.5.20 has too
many bugs, especially when compiling with optimization, as enumerated
(for the most part) in ftp://alpha.gnu.ai.mit.edu/g77.plan.

Version 0.5.21 of g77 is in alpha-testing, but is hung up right now
mainly because I had to return an Alpha system that was loaned to me
over a year ago, and I don't have a suitable replacement yet.

Craftworks kindly donated me a UDB box, I've still got the monitor
I bought for the Alpha loaner (the one I returned), and I've got a
whole bunch of hardware on order to make the UDB box suitable for
further g77 work, but that'll take some time (e.g. once I have the
hardware put together, I have no idea how long it'll take me to
get Linux up and running on it!).  In the meantime the UDB and
nice monitor are just sitting on a shelf in the basement, since
there's nothing I can do with them until I get the external box,
more RAM, and so on.

Also, a serious effort was underway as of a couple of weeks ago to
fund at least half of a "real machine" for development -- "real"
being something substantially faster than a 166MHz UDB, the one
I have right now -- but that effort was kind of put on hold when
somebody offered to donate me a "real machine" outright (which
would mean I wouldn't have to pay 50%).  I haven't heard any more
about that possibility (other than that the donor was considered
quite reliable), so we're all kind of "up in the air", with the
other people who made pledges towards funding 50% of a "real machine"
wondering what is going on (just like me).

(I almost didn't want to mention the outright offer of a "real
machine" to the people willing to fund 50% of one, just to avoid
this situation, plus because I could really make good use of *two*
"real" Alphas *plus* the UDB.  But I didn't feel it would be ethical
to accept funding to get me a "real machine" from people who thought
I needed one and who might not appreciate finding out their funds
went to help buy what amounted to a *second* such machine.)

Fortunately, one of the potential donors for the 50%-funded machine
just sent me a check outright (asking me to keep their identity
anonymous), and another is appying the donation to the cost of the
UDB upgrade I'm ordering (so you can guess who that is -- they're
also giving me great, not-quite-cost-but-close prices on the upgrade
equipment).  That means I'm at least getting some immediate funding
help, even though it's going to the UDB right now instead of the
"real machine" some people (probably rightly) think is really
necessary compared to the UDB.  (At this point, with unexpected
expenses on auto repair plus a fair amount of $$ to upgrade the UDB,
I'm not sure whether I can really afford the 50% of the "real
machine"...but am willing to consider it.)

In the meantime I am doing dribs and drabs of work on g77 using my
i486/33 system (which has neither X nor networking!), so a little
progress is being made, but there *are* known bugs afflicting
Alphas and UltraSPARCs in the 0.5.21 alpha-test version that I have
to address, and cannot work on until my machine situation improves.
(I have access to an Ultra at wee hours of the day via modem, which
has been somewhat helpful.)

Given that 0.5.21 will need at least another week or two of alpha-testing
once I get a new release out with all the fixes, I'd say public release
of 0.5.21 is at *minimum* four weeks away -- say, September 1 -- unless
that promised "real machine" shows up, say, tomorrow, all ready to go
(which seems like wishful thinking).  If the UDB proves problematic (in
terms of getting Linux up and running) and other delays happen (which
seems inevitable), that four weeks will turn into five, six, then three
months, and so on.

What concerns me most about this is that I've promised Cadence I can
return to some semblance of intense (non-g77) work in September, plus
I have made commitments to do some pretty intense work on g77-0.6 as
of very soon, so not having a "real machine" (or even a UDB, which,
compared to my 486/33, *is* real!) means a real window of opportunity
is being wasted in terms of g77 work for Linux/Alpha.

(On the other hand, I am getting some "real work" done getting my
home office in order, fixing things around the house, and so on, so it's
not like I'm *personally* sitting on my hands.  This is probably
just as well; I've let g77 issues pretty much run my life most of
the past 3 or more years, so I don't mind this home-vacation of sorts
at this point.  I'd just enjoy it more if 0.5.21 was out the door
and working; having 0.5.20 as the latest, buggy release is quite
uncomfortable, given that 0.5.19.1, a very stable release, has
no Alpha nor libU77 support, no intrinsics docs, etc.)
