[See <http://www.ccil.org/~esr/writings/cathedral.html> for an explanation
of that term.]

Yeah, me too.

> Note that g77-0.5.21 as it is today would be useful to a lot of people 
> already.

Maybe so!  Wanna release it for me?  ;-)

I do feel that it's gotten to where I'm too much of a bottleneck in
the g77 development process myself...even though there's only a few
people who do g77 coding work *currently*, the number has increased a bit
lately, and I know I've told a number of people that the pending rewrite
of portions for 0.6 might render some of their efforts obsolete...seems
like it's time to stop worrying about that...so maybe there's a number
of people who'd be willing to help out if only I'd get out of their
way (though that'd almost certainly mean I'd have to do some *other*
things to enable them working together).

However, the biggest stumbling-block to putting g77 into bazaar mode
is, IMO, the fact that g77 depends so highly on gcc, and gcc is *not*
in bazaar mode.

In effect, I think putting g77 into bazaar mode would either overload
the ability of g77 developers to cope with the gcc bugs, or result in
g77's version of gcc becoming a "favored" version among a substantial
portion of the non-g77-using population.  I'd love the latter result,
personally, but the FSF probably wouldn't, and it's the former possibility,
among other things, that concerns me -- trying to isolate g77 from
gcc's problems has, as you know, been a substantial challenge and
is maybe the sort of thing the bazaar model wouldn't be too great at
(but it might well be worth a try).

What I think bazaar mode *would* do is allow for rapid improvements
to libf2c at the *very* least, in areas such as performance, porting,
and extensions.  For lots of people, that's the low-hanging-fruit of
g77, and it's probably a lot more scrutable than g77 front-end
or gcc back-end internals.  I've probably got more notifications
of potential or real patches in libf2c than the g77 compiler (front
and back ends combined) at this point, and can't/won't really cope
with them at this point, so these would definitely get plenty of
fresh air quickly if we went into bazaar mode.

Fortunately, some steps I've been taking do have the potential of
loosening things up in the medium term, even though they're rather
frustrating in the short term.  That is, getting my office, computing
machinery, and so on (a new ISP is still in the works) together is
intended to give me a much better base on which to manage g77 stuff,
including possibly setting up automation so I *don't* have to manage
it myself, or can do so with a lot less work.  (E.g. it's hard to
learn about what nifty tools are out there to manage such projects,
e.g. web-page scripting and such, when all I have is a 25x80-character
screen, no X, no printer, and not really enough $$ for a bunch of books
I have little time to read anyway!)

In any case, it seems like switching to bazaar mode *after* a stable
release is out (maybe 0.5.21) is wiser than doing so now, so it's
clearer whether bazaar-mode patches/releases make things worse.

I have *seriously* been considering greatly rolling back my time on
g77 itself and using it instead to design and initiate development
of a new toolset to really make the bazaar development model work
in a "native" way.  Most of our current tools (file systems,
backup and testing procedures, make, diff, patch) actively discourage
the bazaar model -- after all, they were developed primarily to
enable a very few, very knowledgable programmers work in an environment
where they had no need to worry about a bunch of less-experienced,
but enthusiastic, programmers, writers, testers, and so on.  The
WWW (html and script-based) interface is a modest step forward,
but still quite limited in its scope, yet all the basic hardware,
network throughput, and such are available to do things the right
way *now*, and coding is easy enough, so it's just the design work
that is the hard part.

However, it doesn't seem polite for me to change my focus until I
get g77 stable, performing well on Alphas, and (probably) complete
the 0.6 rewrite.  Only an ongoing lack of g77 funding is likely to
cause me to make such a change; even so, I'd probably rather leave
a legacy of a pretty decent g77 if at all possible.

(Given that most of my time debugging and fixing g77 these days
involves bugs in the gcc back end, few of which seem to have
been addressed in the development version, "at all possible" *is*
a serious question at this point.  If it seems unlikely the gcc
development process is going to improve substantially soon, then
it is equally unlikely the current approach to doing g77 -- having
it use the gcc back end -- will produce a decent Fortran compiler.)

How about this: we decide 0.5.21 *will* be released to the public
on September 1, whatever version in alpha-test seems to work best,
regardless of what known bugs (probably fairly obscure, needing
options like -O2 or -fpack-struct) exist?

Then, maybe we could do a new release every 8 weeks or so until we
got one everyone agreed was stable *and* a good performer in the
0.5.x lineage.

Meanwhile, at a suitable point, we could start 0.6 development and
just start releasing those as "very preliminary beta" releases
every N weeks (2, 3, 4, 6, whatever), the idea being that people
wanting "stable" would stick with 0.5.x until told a 0.6.x was
stable?

These are just ideas I'm throwing out; I guess I'd better forward
them to the g77 alpha testers as well, to get their input.

One of the things I was looking forward to in regards a "real Alpha"
to develop on is being able, perhaps, to set it or the UDB up to
regularly run c-torture and whatever g77 tests we develop on
development versions based on patches people submit for that purpose;
and, if I could afford it (if sufficient funding came in), do the
same for a Pentium Pro, maybe this old 486 at least, and maybe someday
an UltraSPARC as well.  As long as I'd have a dedicated machine to
work on myself, I wouldn't mind others getting a decent workout,
and being on the net via a reliable ISP.  Maybe a pipe dream, but
I'm constantly thinking of ways to enable more people to help
work on g77 and other free software with fewer headaches and obstacles.

(I'm remembering what I learned about being a good leader: one
who removes obstacles impeding his followers.  I.e. as a sort of
de-facto project manager of g77, I'd like to get away from telling
people what not to do, and more towards making what they want to
try easier by removing roadblocks such as poor tools, poor ISP access
[really my problem at the moment], no development version of g77
on the net, and so on.  I don't want to become a full-time release
engineer, though, so the current approaches to dealing with these
problems won't work.)
