tplatzer@edusrv.edu.uni-klu.ac.at (KillerLoop) writes:

> we are running a digital multia 233 with digital unix 4.0 ... of course its
> lacking all the nifty features of linux, and i wanted to know how
> compatible to pc-linux an a-linux would be.

Generally speaking, they are very compatible and you'll probably not
notice the difference (except when looking into /proc/cpuinfo... ;-).

Right now, the biggest difference is that Linux/Alpha already made the
switch to an (early version) of GNU libc 2.x.  Linux/x86 is now also
switching to GNU libc 2.x, but until that has happened, you hould
expect some compatibility ripples.  For example, many programs that
use errno do not explicitly include <errno.h>, which is not legal with
GNU libc (for good reasons).

There is, of course, also the issue of 64-bit dirty programs, but in
my experience, that has improved much in recent months, as programmers
become aware that 64-bit architectures are happening.

For binaries that are available in binary form only, the em86 emulator
by Jim Paradis is extremely handy.  I'm afraid a Multia is not quite
up to the task (right now), but on a 300MHz ev5, netscape runs fast
enough to be comfortable to use.  The emulation performance may get
better very soon, but we'll have to wait and see until the new version
of em86 appears.

> i've heard about craftwork-linux and stuff, are they any good? 

Both Craftworks and Red Hat have Linux/Alpha distributions.  I run Red
Hat 4.2, which has some minor, but annoying bugs, like /var/utmp seems
to not being maintained properly at all times and remote printing
still crashes lpd; a fix for the latter is available, though.  These
are bugs that I attribute to the glibc "ripples" I mentioned above.
Overall, I'm  happy with it, though.

