In article <5qoo6h$ho$4@bigboote.WPI.EDU>,
Theo Van Dinter  <felicity@eclectic.kluge.net> wrote:
>I've heard that the motherboards and such which run Alpha Linux are "Low
>end", in comparison to the other Alpha systems out there. (The person
>telling me this is from DEC, so ...)
>
>Does anyone know what the "low end" business is?  Won't take as much RAM?
>ISA slots?  Less memory bandwidth available?

On the UDB, this means:
-- "slow" memory bus. It runs at 33 MHz and is 64 bits large, which is twice
   the bandwidth of a high 486, and the half of a typical pentium system
-- not much cache: only 256 KB on the UDB 166, and 512 KB on the UDB 233;
   1 or 2 MB would provide much better performances
-- modest onboard scsi controller: this is a scsi2 ncr that will not
   handle well more than two scsi2 harddisk
-- little evolutivity: in a UDB, you may only plug 1 PCI card

Other systems exist that do not suffer from the last point (they are indeed
a motherboard with several PCI slots, that fits in a standard PC box).

These "low end" systems may be compared to pentium 100 or 120 systems. With
some programs the cpu will endlessly wait for the memory (look at Quake
for instance), with others it will outperforms any Intel implementation.
Anyway, they are truly very good computers now, as they are really cheap.
In France (where you do not find easily a UDB), there is no computer
cheaper than, say, $900. These systems fill this lack.

	--Thomas Pornin



